\section{Reject Covers That Cannot Be Scheduled}

Check a proposed straight-line cover after matching and before
emitting its instructions. Assume each IR operation belongs to
one selected tile, and that legality checks already establish
the tile's internal ordering and output bindings. Keep directed
dependencies for all value uses and the required memory or
ordering edges. Contract the internal dependencies of each
tile and topologically sort the remaining edges. A cycle
rejects this grouping; changing the order of independent
instructions cannot make it legal under an atomic tile model.

\begin{sudocode}
\Require Operation dependencies $E$, tile map $T$
\Let{G}{\operatorname{graphOfSelectedTiles}(T)}
\ForAll{dependencies $u\to v$ in $E$}
  \To{Internal dependencies are handled by the tile itself.}
  \If{$T[u]\ne T[v]$}
    \State Add edge $T[u]\to T[v]$ to $G$
  \EndIf
\EndFor
\To{Only a tile with no unmet input can issue.}
\Let{Q}{\operatorname{topologicalOrder}(G)}
\If{$Q$ does not contain every tile}
  \State \Return reject this cover
\EndIf
\State \Return schedule $Q$
\end{sudocode}

For input $4$, let $a=x+1=5$, $b=2a=10$, and
$c=a+b=15$, with exact arithmetic on these values.
Suppose a hypothetical instruction can produce both $a$
and $c$ from $x$ and an already available $b$. Grouping
$a$ with $c$ looks like it covers all uses because the tile
exports $a$ and accepts $b$. Yet the instruction computing $b$
needs the grouping's $a$, and the grouping needs that
instruction's $b$. The search must reject this plan.
Three separate tiles allow the order $a,b,c$ and yield $15$.

Every prefix of the returned order has all of its incoming
dependencies inside the prefix. This ensures a compatible
schedule for the contracted dependencies in $O(V+E)$ time,
provided edge bookkeeping handles duplicate dependencies
consistently. The check does not replace type, operand,
effect, or resource checks. If a tile expands into separable
instructions which can expose an intermediate earlier, the
contracted graph is too strong a model; expose those stages
as distinct schedulable units instead.

\section{Whole-Function Graphs}

Ebner and colleagues' \emph{Generalized Instruction Selection using SSA-Graphs} models complex patterns and memory dependencies in a graph grammar with a discrete optimizer. Whole-function selection does not make loop-carried inputs available before the loop: a $\phi$ operand is a use on one incoming edge and is subject to that edge's dominance rules. Also, separate arithmetic matches may be reorderable while an apparent fused load-and-update match is not, owing to aliasing or memory ordering. Keep dependency and placement checks explicit, and compare a heuristic's result on small graphs with an exact, legality-constrained reference. The dossier's \href{https://github.com/Chubek/TheTomeOfLangDev/blob/HEAD/dossier-on-modern-cc-design/chapters/37-compiler-as-a-constraint-solver.md}{Compiler as a Constraint Solver} extends this engineering approach beyond selection.
